knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse  = TRUE,
  comment   = "#>",
  warning   = FALSE,
  message   = FALSE,
  fig.width = 6,
  fig.height = 4,
  dpi       = 150
)
options(flexyBayes.silence_default_prior_note = TRUE)
inla_ok <- requireNamespace("INLA", quietly = TRUE)
brms_ok <- requireNamespace("brms", quietly = TRUE)
```

# 1. Scope

> **This is a syntax-and-representation catalogue.** It demonstrates how to
> *write* models across the term surface, and what each spelling means once
> it reaches an engine. The fits use small sampling budgets so the document
> builds quickly, and several printed diagnostics therefore do **not** meet
> the package's own convergence thresholds
> ($\widehat{R} \le 1.01$, ESS $\ge 400$). Some model classes also mix poorly on
> their backend at any budget. Read the posterior summaries here as
> illustrations of the output *shape*, not as inferential results. For a
> convergence-clean worked fit and the trustworthy-fit workflow, see the
> *getting started* and *cross-engine triangulation* vignettes. The one
> exception is Section 4, whose nested random effect is given a
> production budget: a nested variance component is exactly the kind of
> parameter a short chain reports badly.


`flexybayes()` accepts the asreml-shaped specification

```r
flexybayes(fixed = ..., random = ~ ..., residual = ~ ..., data = ...,
          known_matrices = list(...), weights = ...)
```

with three formulas -- the fixed-effects model matrix, the random-
effects covariance structure, and the residual covariance structure --
plus a named list of pre-computed matrices (kinship, pedigree-derived
numerator relationship, design covariance) and an optional weights
vector. This vignette catalogues every term type the asreml-format
parser accepts, and it is also where the structured-covariance
machinery is documented: heterogeneous residual variance, design
variance components, the several representations a known covariance
can arrive in, and the genotype-by-environment family.

The companion `fb_brms()` entry -- covered in the *getting started*,
*hierarchical models*, and *foundational regression* vignettes --
covers a subset of these term types via brms-style notation. For the
full surface, asreml-shaped formulas are the reference path.

Readers coming from ASReml may want the section "Reading a fit the way
a REML user does" in the *getting started* vignette first. It
translates one field-trial call and the accessors read after it, where
this page catalogues the whole term surface.

> **`flexyBayes` does not depend on the proprietary `asreml` package.**
> The DSL borrows ASReml's notation, and the inference engine is brms
> (Stan / Hamiltonian Monte Carlo) or INLA (Laplace approximation),
> chosen automatically for the model you write. ASReml syntax is
> referenced as a vocabulary, not invoked as a dependency.

We use the bundled `met_example` dataset throughout -- ten genotypes
across four environments with three replicates per cell, plus
genotype-level kinship (`G_mat`) and pedigree-derived (`A_mat`)
matrices.

> **A note on the fits below.** This is a *syntax* reference. Each fit
> exists to show that a term type parses, dispatches to a faithful
> active engine, and returns the expected structure -- not to produce
> publication-grade inference. `backend = "auto"` resolves most terms
> here to INLA (fast, deterministic) or brms (Stan HMC). At these
> modest MCMC budgets the brms-routed fits print convergence
> diagnostics that should not be over-read as inference. The *getting
> started* vignette shows production budgets and how to read
> $\widehat{R}$ and ESS. The *hierarchical models* and *cross-engine
> triangulation* vignettes develop inference quality. Section 6 carries a
> status column for the structured genotype-by-environment terms:
> `diag()`, `us()` and `ar1()` fit, `fa()` and `corh()` are refused by
> name.

```{r met-data}
library(flexyBayes)
set.seed(1100)
data(met_example, package = "flexyBayes")
dat   <- met_example$dat
G_mat <- met_example$G_mat
A_mat <- met_example$A_mat
str(dat, max.level = 1)
```

# 2. Fixed-effect terms

The `fixed` formula is a standard R model formula. Every term that
`stats::model.matrix()` understands is accepted, including:

| Term | Semantics |
|---|---|
| `factor` | factor main effect, with one level absorbed into the intercept |
| `continuous` | continuous covariate, entering as a linear coefficient |
| `factor:factor` | two-factor interaction (factor_interaction internally) |
| `factor:continuous` | factor-continuous interaction (separate slopes) |
| `I(x^2)` | arbitrary I-quoted expression in the predictors |

Two factor-interaction patterns, `*` and `:`, follow R's standard
semantics: `A * B` expands to `A + B + A:B`, and `A:B` is the interaction
alone.

```{r fixed-factor, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
fit_fix <- flexybayes(
  fixed = yield ~ env, data = dat,
  n_samples = 1000, warmup = 2000, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE
)
fit_fix
```

```{r fixed-interaction, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
fit_int <- flexybayes(
  fixed = yield ~ env + x_cov + I(x_cov^2), data = dat,
  n_samples = 1000, warmup = 2000, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE
)
fit_int
```

# 3. Random-effect simple terms

Three closely-related terms designate an iid random effect on a
factor:

| Term | Semantics |
|---|---|
| `simple` (bare factor name in the random formula) | iid normal random effect on a factor |
| `ide(g)` | identical-distribution random effect (synonym of simple) |
| `id(g)` | identity-covariance -- the same as simple |

```{r random-simple, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
fit_rand <- flexybayes(
  fixed     = yield ~ env,
  random    = ~ geno,                       # 'simple' on a bare factor
  data      = dat,
  n_samples = 1000, warmup = 2000, chains = 2, verbose = FALSE
)
fit_rand
```

The random block reports the genotype-level standard deviation. The
companion *hierarchical models* vignette covers the scientific
interpretation. This vignette is about what each spelling means.

# 4. Random-effect interactions and design components

Two compound patterns capture multi-factor random structures:

| Term | Semantics |
|---|---|
| `A:B` (in random) | nested: each level of B within A as its own random level |
| `A + B` (in random) | crossed: independent random effects on A and on B |
| `A + A:B` | hierarchical (A and A-within-B both contribute) |

The example a catalogue should show here is the one a reader will
actually write: genotype main effects plus genotype-by-environment
interaction, the multi-stratum structure of a multi-environment trial.
INLA collapses the finest stratum and refuses it, so `backend = "auto"`
routes to brms, which forms the interaction grouping natively as
`(1 | geno:env)`.

This fit is given a production budget rather than the vignette's usual
small one, because a nested variance component is exactly the kind of
parameter a short chain reports badly:

```{r nested, eval = brms_ok}
fit_nested <- flexybayes(
  fixed     = yield ~ env,
  random    = ~ geno + geno:env,            # genotype-within-environment
  data      = dat,
  n_samples = 2000, warmup = 2000, chains = 4,
  verbose = FALSE, mcmc_verbose = FALSE
)
fit_nested
```

## 4.1 Design variance components (a deliberate identification demonstration)

The fit printed in this section is shown for what its diagnostics do,
not for its estimates. `met_example` has three replicates, which is too
few to estimate a variance from, and a barely-identified variance is
exactly where a sampler's own diagnostics stop being stable: successive
builds of this page, on the same data at the same budget, have printed
$\widehat{R}$ anywhere from 1.005 to 1.241 and a minimum ESS from 13 to
995. Read the badge your own run prints, and quote none of the numbers
below as inference.

A *design* variance component is the same construction applied to the
factors that describe the trial layout: replicates,
blocks-within-replicates, plots-within-blocks. Each layer is added as
an independent random term:

```{r design-vc, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
fit_design <- flexybayes(
  fixed    = yield ~ env,
  random   = ~ rep + rep:block + geno,
  data     = dat,
  n_samples = 1000, warmup = 2000, chains = 4,
  verbose = FALSE, mcmc_verbose = FALSE
)
fit_design
```

The variance-component block reports separate variances for `rep`,
`rep:block`, and `geno`. Each variance is a contribution to total
variance, and the proportions tell you which design layer accounts for
the most variation.

Read the convergence lines on that fit before the estimates. `rep` has
three levels on `met_example`, and a variance estimated from three
levels is barely identified, so this fit's own $\widehat{R}$ and ESS
move a long way between runs at this budget -- read the lines printed by
your run rather than the ones printed here. That is a property of the
design, not of the grammar: the same `rep + rep:block` construction on a
trial with enough replicates behaves like any other random effect.

In field experiments -- incomplete blocks, alpha designs, row-column
designs -- the design variance component is what keeps the standard
errors right. The *spatio-temporal* vignette extends this to spatial
correlation on top of the design structure.

# 5. Random effects with known covariance: `vm()` and `ped()`

`vm(g, K)` lets you supply a pre-computed covariance for the
random-effect levels -- typically a kinship or genomic-relationship
matrix:

```{r vm-kinship, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
fit_vm <- flexybayes(
  fixed     = yield ~ env,
  random    = ~ vm(geno, Gmat),
  data      = dat,
  known_matrices = list(Gmat = G_mat),      # name in formula
  n_samples = 1000, warmup = 2000, chains = 2,
  verbose = FALSE, mcmc_verbose = FALSE
)
fit_vm
```

`ped(g, A)` is the pedigree analogue -- the same structure but with a
numerator-relationship matrix derived from a known pedigree:

```r
flexybayes(
  fixed = yield ~ env,
  random = ~ ped(geno, Amat),
  data = dat,
  known_matrices = list(Amat = A_mat),
  ...
)
```

## 5.1 Carriers: dense, Cholesky, precision, pedigree opt-in

The `vm()` and `ped()` special terms accept the covariance carrier
in multiple representations. The dense $V$ form is the default.
Three additional formats let the caller skip a redundant
factorisation, route through INLA's sparse-precision interface, or
declare a sparse pedigree precision directly. The choice changes the
computational path, not the model.

The covariance carrier is declared with the `fb_cov()` constructor
inside the random term: `vm(group, cov = fb_cov(M, type = ...))`. The
`type` selects how the carrier is interpreted -- `"dense"`,
`"chol"`, `"precision"`, `"blocks"`, or `"low_rank"`. The matrix `M`
names an entry in `known_matrices`, resolved at fit time.

**Dense $V$ (default).** Pass a covariance matrix directly.
`flexyBayes` computes its Cholesky factor at fit time. The bare
`vm(geno, Gmat)` form used above is the dense default, and
`fb_cov(Gmat, type = "dense")` is the explicit equivalent:

```{r vm-dense, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
fit_dense <- flexybayes(
  yield ~ env, random = ~ vm(geno, cov = fb_cov(Gmat, type = "dense")),
  data = dat, known_matrices = list(Gmat = G_mat),
  n_samples = 1000, warmup = 2000, chains = 2,
  verbose = FALSE, mcmc_verbose = FALSE
)
fit_dense
```

**User-supplied Cholesky factor.** If the lower-triangular Cholesky
factor of $V$ is already available (serialised from a prior step, or
computed once across many fits), declare it with `fb_cov(L, type =
"chol")` to skip the `t(chol(V))` recomputation:

```r
L_user <- t(chol(G_mat))   # build once, reuse across fits
flexybayes(
  yield ~ env, random = ~ vm(geno, cov = fb_cov(L_user, type = "chol")),
  data = dat, known_matrices = list(L_user = L_user)
)
```

The chol input must be square and lower-triangular. The validator
refuses on an upper-triangular or non-square input.

**Sparse precision matrix.** Many sources of structured covariance
arrive sparse upstream of `flexyBayes`: pedigree-based $A^{-1}$ is
sparse by construction, spatial precision matrices from BYM2 / ICAR
are sparse, and many block-independent designs have sparse precision
even when the covariance itself is dense. Passing the sparse
precision directly avoids dense intermediates, and routes through
INLA's user-defined precision interface `f(<var>_id, model =
"generic0", Cmatrix = Q)` (Rue & Held 2005, *Gaussian Markov Random
Fields*, and Lindgren et al. 2011):

```{r vm-precision, eval = inla_ok}
Q <- solve(G_mat)   # or build sparse Q directly via ICAR / BYM2
fit_prec <- flexybayes(
  yield ~ env, random = ~ vm(geno, cov = fb_cov(Q, type = "precision")),
  data = dat, known_matrices = list(Q = Q),
  backend = "inla", verbose = FALSE
)
fit_prec
```

The sparse path is the recommended route for large spatial / genomic
problems where the dense covariance would not fit in memory. The
validator checks symmetry and positive-definiteness, and refusals name
the failure mode (`precision_not_symmetric`,
`precision_not_positive_definite`).

**Pedigree sparse-precision opt-in.** When the user has already
inverted the pedigree to $A^{-1}$ (or pulled sparse $A^{-1}$
directly from `pedigreemm::getAInv()`), the `ped()` constructor
takes a flag that routes through the sparse-precision codepath
without a redundant inversion:

```r
A_inv <- solve(A_mat)   # or pedigreemm::getAInv(ped_obj)
flexybayes(
  yield ~ env,
  random = ~ ped(animal, cov = fb_cov(A_inv, type = "precision",
                                      sparse_precision = TRUE)),
  data = animal_data, known_matrices = list(A_inv = A_inv),
  backend = "inla"
)
```

`flexyBayes` does not depend on `pedigreemm` or any pedigree-graph
library. The user constructs $A^{-1}$ and passes it. The level
ordering of the grouping factor must match the row/column order of
the supplied precision matrix.

**One carrier per term.** A single `vm()` term carries exactly one
`fb_cov()` declaration. The five carrier types -- `"dense"`,
`"chol"`, `"precision"`, `"blocks"`, `"low_rank"` -- are mutually
exclusive within a carrier. The bare dense form `vm(geno, Gmat)`
remains available as the default when no `cov =` is supplied.

**Backend mapping.** With `backend = "auto"`, dispatch routes
precision-type vm/ped to INLA (preferred for sparsity), and chol- and
dense-type vm/ped to brms.

## 5.2 Known covariance alongside design components

Sections 4.1 and 5.1 compose: a `vm()` genotype term sits alongside
ordinary design random effects in the same model.

```r
flexybayes(
  fixed  = yield ~ env,
  random = ~ rep + rep:block + vm(geno, cov = fb_cov(Gmat, type = "dense")),
  data = dat, known_matrices = list(Gmat = G_mat)
)
```

# 6. Genotype-by-environment structured covariance

> **Where genotype-by-environment covariance stands.** The diagonal
> family (`diag(env):geno`, and its `idh()` / `at()` spellings) and the
> unstructured `us(env):geno` fit on brms. INLA refuses both by name.
> The factor-analytic `fa(env, k):geno` parses for the formula
> catalogue and no active engine emits it. The rest of this section
> gives the whole family with the refusal you get where there is no
> route, and the identification limit that matters more than the
> syntax.

Six term types *parse* structured covariance among random-effect
levels, typically genotype-by-environment in agricultural settings --
the standard multi-environment-trial parameterisations (Smith, Cullis,
& Thompson, 2001). Four of them fit today, and the status column says
which:

| Term | Semantics | Status |
|---|---|---|
| `diag(env):geno` | diagonal: each environment has its own genotype variance, no cross-environment correlation | fits (brms) |
| `idh(env):geno`, `at(env):geno` | ASReml's other spellings for the same structure | fits (brms) |
| `us(env):geno` | unstructured: the full $n(n+1)/2$-parameter environment-by-environment covariance for genotype effects | fits (brms) |
| `corh(env):geno` | heterogeneous variances with one shared correlation | refused by name |
| `fa(env, k):geno` | factor-analytic: a rank-`k` approximation, $\boldsymbol{\Sigma} = \boldsymbol{\Lambda}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}^\top + \boldsymbol{\Psi}$ | refused by name |
| `ar1(env):geno` | autoregressive across environments, independent across genotypes | fits (INLA), one observation per cell |

INLA refuses the diagonal and unstructured members, so
`backend = "auto"` routes them to brms. Both lower to a brms
group-level term over `geno` carrying one coefficient per
environment: uncorrelated for `diag()`, correlated for `us()`.

```{r us-gxe, eval = brms_ok}
fit_us <- flexybayes(
  fixed     = yield ~ env,
  random    = ~ us(env):geno,
  data      = dat,
  backend   = "brms",
  n_samples = 1000, warmup = 2000, chains = 2,
  verbose = FALSE, mcmc_verbose = FALSE
)
fit_us
```

It mixes acceptably here, on four environments with three replicates
per cell. The structure gets hard fast, and the reason is
identification rather than budget.

**Identification matters more than the syntax here.** An
unstructured `us(env):geno` estimates $n$ standard deviations and
$n(n-1)/2$ correlations alongside a residual variance. With one
observation per genotype-environment cell -- the usual shape of a
published multi-environment-trial summary -- the residual variance is
confounded with the diagonal of that covariance, and the two are not
separately identified. The sampler shows it: on
`agridat::yan.winterwheat` (18 genotypes, 9 environments, one plot per
cell) the 45 covariance parameters converge cleanly while `sigma`
reaches $\widehat{R}$ 1.13 at an effective size of 36, with divergent
transitions, and doubling the budget makes every one of those worse
rather than better. The covariance is then conditional on a residual
the chains disagree about. Fit `us()` on data with replication within
cell, or put an informative prior on the residual, or use the diagonal
structure, which is identified from the same data. Sampler tuning
reaches Stan through `control` -- `control = list(adapt_delta = 0.95)`
is the standard response to divergent transitions -- and it lowers the
divergence count on this model, which is worth doing once the split is
identified and misleading before it: the divergences are the sampler
reporting the ridge, not causing it. The *multi-environment trials and
genomic selection* vignette meets the same ridge on a real series.

The factor-analytic and equicorrelated forms parse into the catalogue and
are refused by name, with the alternative stated:

```{r fa-refusal, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
refused <- tryCatch(
  flexybayes(
    fixed  = yield ~ env,
    random = ~ fa(env, 2):geno,
    data   = dat,
    verbose = FALSE
  ),
  error = function(e) e
)
cat("Refused:", conditionMessage(refused), "\n")
```

`ar1(env):geno` is a different animal from the other five. It is an
autoregressive *field* over the environment ordering, emitted on INLA as
a latent AR1 grouped independently by genotype. Being a field, it needs
one observation per (environment, genotype) node, which `met_example`
does not have: it carries three replicates per cell.

```{r ar1-gxe-refusal, eval = inla_ok}
refused_ar1 <- tryCatch(
  flexybayes(
    fixed   = yield ~ env,
    random  = ~ ar1(env):geno,
    data    = dat,
    backend = "inla",
    verbose = FALSE
  ),
  error = function(e) e
)
cat("Refused:", conditionMessage(refused_ar1), "\n")
```

The *spatio-temporal* vignette fits the same machinery on a field grid,
where one plot per node is the norm.

# 7. Splines and polynomials

| Term | Semantics |
|---|---|
| `spl(x)` | cubic smoothing spline on `x` |
| `spl(x, type = "bs", df = m)` | random B-spline with `m` basis functions |
| `spl(x, type = "ps", df = m)` | penalised B-spline (P-spline) |
| `pol(x, degree = d)` | polynomial of degree `d` (random coefficients) |

Splines and polynomials live in the `random` formula because their
coefficients are penalised -- equivalent to a random effect with a
smoothing-induced covariance. See the *foundational regression*
vignette for examples.

```r
flexybayes(fixed = y ~ 1, random = ~ spl(x, type = "ps", df = 15),
          data = dat, ...)
```

# 8. Residual covariance: `residual`

The `residual` formula describes structure on the residual error term.
The default -- unstated `residual` -- is iid Gaussian with a single
$\sigma_e$.

| Term | Semantics |
|---|---|
| `~ units` (default) | iid residual |
| `~ at(env):units`, `~ dsum(~ units \| env)` | heterogeneous residual variance per environment |
| `~ ar1(row):ar1(col)` | ASReml's separable autoregressive residual -- **refused by name** |

## 8.1 Heterogeneous residual variance

Distinct from a genotype-by-environment covariance restriction,
heterogeneous *residual* variance is the model in which $\sigma_e^2$
varies across some grouping factor -- almost always true in field-trial
data, where a wet environment is noisier than a dry one:

```{r het-residual, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
fit_het <- flexybayes(
  fixed    = yield ~ env,
  random   = ~ geno,
  residual = ~ at(env):units,
  data     = dat,
  n_samples = 1000, warmup = 2000, chains = 2,
  verbose = FALSE, mcmc_verbose = FALSE
)
fit_het
```

The printed fit carries a *Residual by level of `env`* block: one
residual variance and one residual standard deviation per environment,
each a posterior median with a 95% credible interval.

Read the block, not the coefficients. brms represents a sectioned
residual as a linear predictor on **log** sigma, `sigma ~ 0 + env`, so
the coefficient `summary()` lists as `sigma_envE2` (brms calls it
`b_sigma_envE2`) is neither a variance nor a standard deviation -- the
variance for that level is $\exp(2b)$ and the standard deviation is
$\exp(b)$. Both are summarised here from the draws of those transforms
rather than by transforming a posterior mean, which is a different
number because $\exp$ is convex. There is also no scalar $\sigma$ left
in the model: the sectioning replaces the residual scale rather than
sitting beside it, which is why the variance-component table above the
block does not list one.

`summary()` prints the same block alongside the fixed effects and the
variance components:

```{r het-residual-summary, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
summary(fit_het)
```

In field-trial data, ignoring residual heterogeneity biases the
genotype-effect posteriors toward the noisier environments. The mapping
is checked against ASReml's `dsum(~ units | site)` and against
`nlme::varIdent()` on simulated data before it is used: the two REML
implementations agree to 1.3e-07, every arm fits one free scale per
level with no covariance, and the fit through `flexybayes()` returns
per-site variances within 4.8% of the REML estimates.

## 8.2 The separable autoregressive residual, and why it refuses

The last row of the table above is the one place in this catalogue
where a term ASReml accepts is refused rather than fitted, and the
reason is worth reading before comparing the two tools on a field
trial. ASReml's separable residual is a single correlated residual with
no independent plot error: three parameters on a row-by-column grid.
What INLA emits is a latent autoregressive field *plus* the Gaussian
observation nugget: four parameters, containing ASReml's model as the
nugget goes to zero, and returning different correlations from the same
data whenever there is real plot-to-plot noise. The field is therefore
written where it belongs, on the random side:

```r
flexybayes(fixed = yield ~ rep, random = ~ geno + ar1(row):ar1(col),
          data = field_data, ...)
```

That spelling needs a complete grid with one observation per (row,
column) node. An incomplete or replicated grid refuses rather than being
approximated, and the default `na_action = "augment"` keeps unharvested
plots as design cells so a trial with missing plots is still fittable.
The *spatio-temporal* vignette demonstrates all of this, including the
four recovered hyperparameters against a known simulation truth.

# 9. The `weights` argument

The intended meaning is the ASReml one: a non-negative weight vector
scaling the residual variance, $y_i \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_i, \sigma_e^2 /
w_i)$, which is useful when observations are pre-aggregated (cell means,
weighted averages, prior counts).

**It is not yet supported.** The argument is parsed and recorded in the
model representation, but neither active emitter consumes it, so a
non-constant weight vector is refused rather than fitted:

```r
flexybayes(fixed = ..., data = ..., weights = w_i, ...)
#> Error: `weights` are not yet supported by the active backends
```

The refusal is deliberate. Both backends previously generated identical
code with and without weights, so a weighted call returned the
unweighted posterior with nothing to indicate it. The word also covers
several distinct models -- inverse-residual-variance, frequency,
likelihood-power, trials, exposure -- which cannot share one argument
without saying which is meant, so each mapping is being implemented and
validated against an analytic oracle before the argument is honoured.

# 10. The `family` and `link` arguments

For non-Gaussian responses, set `family` and (optionally) override
the canonical `link`:

| `family` | Default `link` |
|---|---|
| `"gaussian"` | identity |
| `"binomial"` | logit |
| `"poisson"` | log |
| `"negative_binomial"` | log |
| `"gamma"` | log |
| `"beta"` | logit |

```r
flexybayes(fixed = y ~ x, random = ~ g, family = "binomial",
          link = "probit", data = ..., ...)
```

# 11. Pitfalls

**Factor inference inside the formula.** `flexyBayes` factor-coerces
character columns automatically, but does *not* infer ordering. If
your factor levels carry a natural order (low / medium / high), set
the levels explicitly.

**Match `known_matrices` keys to formula names.** The string keys of
`known_matrices` must literally match the symbol names used inside
`vm()` / `ped()` calls. A common mistake is `vm(geno, G_mat)` with
`known_matrices = list(Gmat = G_mat)` -- the name in the formula is
`G_mat`, not `Gmat`. Either rename the formula symbol or rename the
list key, but they must match.

**`spl()` and `pol()` are random-effect terms.** Writing
`fixed = y ~ spl(x)` will throw `could not find function "spl"`
because `model.frame()` evaluates the formula and there is no
exported `spl` function. The correct invocation puts smoothers in
`random`.

**A factor-analytic GxE model has no active fitting route (Section 6).**
This is the consequence of the greta quarantine most likely to affect a
multi-environment-trial analysis. The diagonal and unstructured members
of the family do fit, on brms.

**Reading a sectioned residual off the coefficients.** `b_sigma_E2` is a
coefficient on log sigma. Squaring it, or comparing it with an ASReml
`env_2!R`, compares a log-scale coefficient with a variance. Use the
*Residual by level* block of Section 8.1.

**Boundary on a heterogeneous-residual fit.** When one environment is
much less variable than the others, the corresponding residual posterior
can pile up near zero. The default uniform-on-SD prior does not push it
away. A long-tailed prior (lognormal) on the residual would, and the
posterior would then say more about the prior than about the data.

**Convergence on small datasets with rich covariances.** A design with
many nested random terms and few observations per cell will not identify
all of them, and the unstructured GxE case in Section 6 is the sharpest
version of that. Convergence diagnostics flag it. Drop the
least-supported term, or add the replication that identifies it, rather
than trusting an unidentified posterior.

# 12. Active prompts

1. Refit `met_example` with `random = ~ vm(geno, Amat)` (pedigree
   relationship matrix) and compare the genotype-level posteriors
   with the kinship version.
2. Fit `random = ~ us(env):geno` (Section 6) and then
   `random = ~ diag(env):geno` on the same data. Compare the number of
   variance parameters each reports, and the mixing diagnostics. Which
   difference is the structure, and which is the budget?
3. Refit `fixed-interaction` (Section 2) with `backend = "inla"` and
   `backend = "brms"` explicitly instead of `"auto"`. Do the two
   posteriors agree?
4. Refit `met_example` with `residual = ~ at(env):units` versus
   `residual = ~ units` (homogeneous). How much does the genotype
   posterior spread change?
5. Read the three variance components of the design fit in Section 4.1
   against each other. Which design layer dominates, and does its
   interval separate from zero at three replicate levels?
6. Fit the same `vm(geno, Gmat)` model via the dense and the
   precision (`solve(Gmat)`) carriers. Confirm the posteriors agree.

# 13. Companion: brms-shaped formulas

Every model above is specified in the asreml grammar
(`fixed` / `random`). The same models can equally be written in the
lme4 / brms idiom, with the random structure carried in `(1 | g)`
blocks. The universal entry accepts either grammar and auto-detects
which one you used:

```{r fb-brms-shape, eval = inla_ok || brms_ok}
fit_b <- flexybayes(
  yield ~ env + (1 | geno),     # brms-shaped; the same model as
                                # fixed = yield ~ env, random = ~ geno
  data         = dat,
  n_samples    = 1000,
  warmup       = 2000,
  chains       = 2,
  verbose      = FALSE,
  mcmc_verbose = FALSE
)
fit_b
```

The supported brms-shaped corpus is deliberately narrow: fixed
effects plus single or crossed random intercepts, on Gaussian,
binomial (single-column Bernoulli), or Poisson responses. Five
representative shapes:

| Shape | Form |
|---|---|
| Gaussian, fixed only | `y ~ x1 + x2` |
| Gaussian random intercept | `y ~ x + (1 \| g)` |
| Crossed random intercepts | `y ~ x + (1 \| g1) + (1 \| g2)` |
| Bernoulli random intercept | `y ~ x + (1 \| g)` with `family = "binomial"` |
| Poisson random intercept | `count ~ x + (1 \| g)` with `family = "poisson"` |

Constructs outside this corpus -- correlated random slopes, smoothers
(`s()`), Gaussian processes, autocorrelation (`ar()`, `ma()`),
multivariate or hurdle / mixture families, and LHS addition forms
(`y | trials(n) ~ ...`) -- raise a structured refusal at ingest that
names both the offending construct and the closest supported
alternative. An *uncorrelated* random slope, `(x || g)`, is inside the
corpus and is the spelling to reach for. The correlated `(x | g)` refuses
and says so. Do not translate a slope into the asreml crossing
`random = ~ g + g:x` with numeric `x`: that is one independent deviation
per cell rather than a per-group slope, and flexyBayes refuses the
spelling rather than choosing between the two readings. The *understanding
dispatch and refusals* and *cross-engine triangulation* vignettes catalogue the
principled boundaries.

Backend choice is orthogonal to grammar. The universal entries
`flexybayes()` / `fb()` take a `backend = c("auto", "inla", "brms")`
argument (see the *hierarchical models* vignette for the dispatch flow).
The engine pins `fb_inla()` and `fb_brms()` fix the backend instead.
`backend = "greta"` is still accepted as a value and raises a
`backend_quarantined` refusal: greta is retained as a re-entry candidate,
not an active engine. Either grammar reaches the same cross-engine
post-fit surface -- `triangulate()` and the registry-driven canonical
parameter-name resolver (no `name_map` is needed for the common
fixed-effect + random-intercept cases).

